Integrating Foundation Models and Feature-Based Neural Networks for DNA Barcoding Classification

The "Dataset" folder contains the datasets used in this paper (see the related Dataset/README for details of the datasets), given the size constraints, 
the only datasets not uploaded directly are the two datasets Insecta, 
which you can download directly from the Zenodo link: https://zenodo.org/records/13858306 The original documentation does not include a description of the 
all_string_dnas field, it directly contains the DNA strings. Dataset/README also explains how to create the two datasets, InsectaG and InsectaS.

We used accuracy and the error under the ROC curve (EUC) as performance indicators. The EUC (1- Area Uder the ROC curve) was computed using MATLAB’s official rocmetrics function.
For InsectG and InsectaS the weighted accuracy is adopted. Specifically, the accuracy
value for each class is normalized by the number of samples belonging to that class, as suggested in the literature.

"eDNA" folder contains the code for extracting the features using the methods that built eDNA: 2-hot (section 2.2.3) and chaos game based approach (section 2.2.1);

The “ToolWavelet2026” folder contains the code for the "2-hot wavelet" method, described in Section 2.2.3 of the paper.

The "3DCV_Code" folder contains the code for 3D-CGR - Vision Transformers, described in Section 2.2.2 and 2.3.1 of the paper. Remember that if you use the logits produced by this approach and then want to combine them
with another network whose output has already been normalized using Softmax, you should first apply Softmax 
(or another suitable normalization method) to the 3DCV logits. 
More generally, always verify that the score ranges of the outputs you are combining are comparable; otherwise, the fusion may be biased.

IngaTest/TrainV2.fas: Corrected version of the Inga dataset, we identified typographical errors in the reference version which we believe should be
reported. In particular, some vertical bars (“ | ”) were mistakenly replaced with underscores (“_”), preventing the
correct separation of species names from sequence identifiers. This resulted in incorrect species labels and altered
the total number of classes, which may affect also the reported results. 

